The CLB has an onerous duty to form an opinion with regard to the existence of an intent to defraud before ordering investigation under section 237(b). The facts and circumstances of the instant case prima facie demonstrated and established the existence of pre-requisites, which compelled to form an opinion in terms of section 237(b). On a consideration of the facts and circumstances of the case, the grounds for ordering investigation did exist. There was an overwhelming material pointing to the circumstances suggesting that the business of the company was conducted for a fraudulent and unlawful purpose with the intent to defraud the general public and the other body corporate and the persons concerned in the management of the company had been guilty of fraud, misfeasance or other misconduct towards the company. The instant case was a case squarely covered under the provisions of section 237(b) as the pre-conditions for initiating such an action existed as would be clear from the following:—

(i)STL, an artificial person, divorced from its shareholders, seemed to have been cheated. Consequently, it had suffered a loss of more than Rs. 104 crore as reflected in the books of account. Shareholders were not the company. Company was a separate legal entity whose interest itself seemed to have been compromised by paying sale consideration to the shareholders. This, in itself, was a sufficient ground for ordering investigation under section 237(b). This was a very unusual case where the consideration for taking over of an unlisted company instead of being paid to the company had been passed on to the shareholders of STL. It had been rightly pointed out that in the event of dispute, the shareholders of STL could face difficulty in enforcing that agreement in the Court of Law because the principle of privity of contract would come into play;

(ii)The general public seemed to have been cheated. The business of the company seemed to have been conducted with an intent to defraud its creditors and public at large. In the wake of market manipulations in the scrips, as clearly brought out by the SEBI, fraudulent means appeared to have been adopted violative of the SEBI Act and Regulations to defraud the creditors and public at large. The facts as stated pointed out towards the involvement of companies belonging to the Ketan Parekh Group and the stock scam preliminarily revealed by the SEBI. The modus operandi, in the instant case, could be traced out only by investigation under section 237(b);

(iii)The takeover of the unlisted company, as admitted by the respondents, was pursuant to the due diligence report of a reputed legal firm. The report specifically and elaborately explained the basis and the procedure to be followed and also confirmed that the entire transaction was in accordance with the law. The respondents relied on that report. They could not choose to deny the following state of affairs as contained in that report:

The respondent-company had caused the cancellation of its sales tax registration;

The respondent-company had not obtained the necessary certificate from the appropriate authority under the Shops and Establishment Act;

The respondent-company did not presently have any employees and, therefore, did not have any registered code number under the Provident Fund or the Employee State Insurance legislations;

The respondent-company did not apply for registration as an NBFC even when it was carrying out NBFC activities.

The respondent could not choose to rely on one part and ignore the other. The above extract made the credentials and intention of the respondent-company amply clear. Such credentials raised suspicion and the question, as to why a company like STL agreed to sell its business undertaking to a company like the respondent-company and suffered loss, remained unanswered. Raising of share capital from Rs. 2.5 crore to Rs. 25 crore in violation of section 97 could not go unnoticed. Investigation seemed to be the remedy available.

(iv )The directors owe fiduciary duty to the shareholders and they have to function in not only in good faith but in very good faith. But in the instant case, shareholders’ faith seemed to have been belied. It was a case where the respondents and the persons concerned in the management of the company appeared to be guilty of fraud, misfeasance and other misconduct towards the company. The business of the company appeared to have been carried on otherwise for a fraudulent and unlawful purpose. There was no force in the respondents’ plea that the condition precedent for exercising power under section 237(b) is that the business of the company should be in operation, i.e., the company should be carrying on operation and in case by any reason whatsoever has stopped carrying on the business, then no investigation can be carried out.

