14.13-1b Judicial remedies - Of the other remedies of a judicial nature available to members, these are divided into what an individual member may proceed against the company on his initiative and as group actions.

Individual Remedies

14.13-2 The first kind of individual remedies include : (1) any member, past or present, to apply to the Court or Tribunal that has convicted a person of any offence in connection with the promotion, formation or management of the company to restrain such a person from managing the company if he attempts to do so, section 203;

(2) if the holding of any general meeting is impracticable, any member entitled to vote may petition the Company Law Board 1 /Tribunal to order the holding of a general meeting, section 186;

(3) any member may apply to the Central Government or the Company Law Board 1 /Tribunal with a plea to investigate the affairs of the company by the Central Government Inspectors, if circumstances exist suggesting fraud in the management of the company or misfeasance or misconduct towards the company or any of its member or members, or that the members of the company have not been given all the information with respect to the company’s affairs that is reasonably expected including the payment of managerial remuneration, section 237;

(4) any member to complain to the Company Law Board 1 /Tribunal seeking imposition of the restrictions under section 250;

(5) any member to apply to the Company Law Board 1/Tribunal for an order of winding up of the company by the Company Law Board 1 / Tribunal, sections 433 and 439;

10.7 Lifting of the Veil - Instances of lifting corporate veil under the provisions of the Act

Examples in the Act itself

10.7-1 At least fifteen instances where the corporate veil is lifted disregarding the corporate personality of a company are found in the Act itself:

u Section 4 giving the meaning of ‘holding company’ and ‘subsidiary’ allowing one company to have control on the composition of the board of directors of another company thereby making its own subsidiary company;

u Section 212 requiring that the annual report of the subsidiary companies shall be attached to the balance sheet of the holding company in addition to giving certain other particulars pertaining to the interest of the holding company in the subsidiaries in its annual report each year (as group accounts);

u Section 214(1) conferring rights on the holding company’s representatives authorized by its resolution (whether of board or of the general body) to inspect the books of account kept by any of its subsidiaries at any time during business hours;

u Section 214(2) conferring the right to move the Company Law Board allowed to the members of a company under section 235 on the members of the holding company as if they alone were members of the subsidiary seeking its order that the affairs of the subsidiary ought to be investigated by an inspector or inspectors at which the Central Government shall be bound to appoint one or more competent persons as inspectors to investigate the affairs of the subsidiary and to report thereon as directed by the Central Government;

u Section 45 rendering the residuary members in a company severally liable for the whole debts made by a company in a period of more than six months during which the company carried on business with the number of members reduced to less than the number prescribed as the legal minimum in the knowledge of the residuary members;

u Sections 81(4) and 94A, automatic increase of share capital by the order of Central Government in respect of conversion of Government loans to companies into shares irrespective of any option reserved for it or loans granted by Public Financial Institutions to companies with such option;

u Section 235 under which the powers of investigation of the affairs of a company are conferred on the Central Government;

u Section 408 conferring power on the Central Government to appoint its nominees as directors on the board of directors of a company in connection with prevention of oppression and mismanagement;

u Section 409 conferring power on the Company Law Board to prevent change in the board of directors alleged as likely to affect the company prejudicially by the managing director, any director or manager of the company;

u Section 424 providing for appointment of Receivers or Managers of companies by the Courts or under instruments for loans;

u Power of lenders to appoint one or more of their nominees as directors on the boards of companies during the term of the loan agreements (extended permissive use of Sections 260 and 262) compulsively;

u Section 147(4) fastening fine and personal liability of the issuer to the holder of the negotiable instrument signed on behalf of the company with mis-description of the company’s name affixed on it;

u Section 542 providing for liability of persons carrying on fraudulent and wrongful trading in the name of the company while it is insolvent and about to be wound up;

u Section 583 providing for a foreign company being liable to be wound up as an un-registered company, and

u Section 396 reserving the power to the Central Government to order amalgamation of companies in national interest.

Lifting of corporate veil by courts

10.7-2 There may be circumstances that compel the Courts in their discretion to go beyond and reach to the director(s) or member(s) personally in their examination of the realities, and when this takes place it is said that the corporate veil or mask is lifted. In TELCO v. State of Bihar AIR 1965 SC 40, the Supreme Court observed:

"......... the doctrine, that a corporation or a company has legal and separate entity of its own has been subjected to certain exceptions by the application of the fiction that the veil of the corporation can be lifted and its face examined in substance. The doctrine of the lifting of the veil thus marks a change in the attitude that the law had originally adopted towards the concept of the separate entity or personality of the corporation. As a result of the impact of the complexity of the economic factors, judicial decisions have sometimes recognized exceptions to the rule, about the juristic personality of the corporation. It may be that in course of time these exceptions may grow in number and to meet the requirements of different economic problems, the theory, about the personality of the corporation may be confined more and more.

