held.text:
Prima facie it appears that respondent No. 1 and such other companies, which are mushrooming in the country under the corporate veil, are committing fraud by tempting middle class persons to invest their lifelong savings in the high hope of having fanciful returns, without there being any basis. Further, in agriculture productions, this type of high returns are impossibility in this country, where, otherwise, the farmers of this country would have become multi-millionaires by this time. It appears that, because of this empty, false promises, investors are duped and the company’s moneys are siphoned off either to sister companies or firms of the directors or their relatives.

As held in DDA v. Skipper Construction Co. (P.) Ltd. AIR 1996 SCW 2401 where the corporate character is employed for the purpose of committing illegality or for defrauding others, the Court would ignore the entity with regard to corporate company and its association of men and women and will do justice between the real persons. The concept of corporate entity was evolved to encourage and promote trade and commerce but not to commit illegalities or to defraud people. Absence of a statutory provision will not inhibit the Court while acting under article 142/226 from making appropriate orders for doing complete justice between the parties. In India, the Courts are not only Courts of law, but also the Courts of equity. In the aforesaid case, the Court passed an appropriate order attaching certain properties of the Directors of the company.

Applying the aforesaid ratio and taking into consideration the fact that large number of investors are being defrauded by employing the corporate veil, which may amount to a criminal offence, and also the fact that under Criminal Law property acquired by fraud, cheating, etc., can be followed and seized from wherever it is lying, appropriate directions could be issued by the Court to search and seize such property. Further, under article 226 of the Constitution, the Court can issue appropriate directions for not only enforcing any of the rights conferred by Part III but ‘for any other purpose’, and ‘any other purpose’ would include such public interest purpose.

In this view of the matter, following order was passed—

(a)The D.G.P. of State of Maharashtra was directed to instruct the State CID, Poona and other concerned police authorities to enquire into various frauds committed by the first respondent-company and its directors.

(b)Investigating Officers were directed to hold necessary enquiry to find out the whereabouts of the first respondent-company’s property, both movable and immovable, and whereabouts of its directors who were not traceable.

(c)Respondent Nos. 1 to 8 and the group companies were directed to forthwith state on oath, in an affidavit the following:

(i )The list of their movable and immovable properties and all assets and location thereof;

(ii )The list of their bank accounts and the particulars thereof, including the names and addresses of the banks and amounts therein;

(d)Respondent Nos. 2 to 8 were directed to furnish a list of group companies and indicate the amounts and assets, whether movable or immovable, transferred from respondent No. 1 to its group companies or any other companies in which respondent Nos. 2 to 8 were directors or shareholders;

(e)Respondent No. 9 was directed to write to all scheduled commercial banks, intimating the present order and not to permit respondent Nos. 1 to 8 and the group companies of respondent No. 1 to operate their respective bank accounts;

(f) Investigating Officers were further directed to find out the assets of the directors of the first respondent-company and whether any amount of the first respondent-company was siphoned off to any sister concerns or in the names of relatives of the directors; if necessary by taking assistance of Auditors;

(g )Investigating Officers were directed to prepare an inventory of the properties belonging to respondent Nos. 1 to 8 and the group companies mentioned in page 131 of the petition and to attach them. This was for the time being, to find out whether the properties belonging to the first respondent-company were siphoned off by the directors by defrauding the investors;

(h) The Directors of the first respondent-company were directed to co-operate with the aforesaid enquiry;

(i )Investigating Officers enquiring into the matter would only attach the property and not dispossess the persons in possession thereof after preparing the necessary panchanama; and

(j )Investigating Officers were directed to submit their interim report on or before 14-12-1998 under a sealed cover.

The CLB has an onerous duty to form an opinion with regard to the existence of an intent to defraud before ordering investigation under section 237(b). The facts and circumstances of the instant case prima facie demonstrated and established the existence of pre-requisites, which compelled to form an opinion in terms of section 237(b). On a consideration of the facts and circumstances of the case, the grounds for ordering investigation did exist. There was an overwhelming material pointing to the circumstances suggesting that the business of the company was conducted for a fraudulent and unlawful purpose with the intent to defraud the general public and the other body corporate and the persons concerned in the management of the company had been guilty of fraud, misfeasance or other misconduct towards the company. The instant case was a case squarely covered under the provisions of section 237(b) as the pre-conditions for initiating such an action existed as would be clear from the following:—

(i)STL, an artificial person, divorced from its shareholders, seemed to have been cheated. Consequently, it had suffered a loss of more than Rs. 104 crore as reflected in the books of account. Shareholders were not the company. Company was a separate legal entity whose interest itself seemed to have been compromised by paying sale consideration to the shareholders. This, in itself, was a sufficient ground for ordering investigation under section 237(b). This was a very unusual case where the consideration for taking over of an unlisted company instead of being paid to the company had been passed on to the shareholders of STL. It had been rightly pointed out that in the event of dispute, the shareholders of STL could face difficulty in enforcing that agreement in the Court of Law because the principle of privity of contract would come into play;

(ii)The general public seemed to have been cheated. The business of the company seemed to have been conducted with an intent to defraud its creditors and public at large. In the wake of market manipulations in the scrips, as clearly brought out by the SEBI, fraudulent means appeared to have been adopted violative of the SEBI Act and Regulations to defraud the creditors and public at large. The facts as stated pointed out towards the involvement of companies belonging to the Ketan Parekh Group and the stock scam preliminarily revealed by the SEBI. The modus operandi, in the instant case, could be traced out only by investigation under section 237(b);

(iii)The takeover of the unlisted company, as admitted by the respondents, was pursuant to the due diligence report of a reputed legal firm. The report specifically and elaborately explained the basis and the procedure to be followed and also confirmed that the entire transaction was in accordance with the law. The respondents relied on that report. They could not choose to deny the following state of affairs as contained in that report:

The respondent-company had caused the cancellation of its sales tax registration;

The respondent-company had not obtained the necessary certificate from the appropriate authority under the Shops and Establishment Act;

The respondent-company did not presently have any employees and, therefore, did not have any registered code number under the Provident Fund or the Employee State Insurance legislations;

The respondent-company did not apply for registration as an NBFC even when it was carrying out NBFC activities.

The respondent could not choose to rely on one part and ignore the other. The above extract made the credentials and intention of the respondent-company amply clear. Such credentials raised suspicion and the question, as to why a company like STL agreed to sell its business undertaking to a company like the respondent-company and suffered loss, remained unanswered. Raising of share capital from Rs. 2.5 crore to Rs. 25 crore in violation of section 97 could not go unnoticed. Investigation seemed to be the remedy available.

(iv )The directors owe fiduciary duty to the shareholders and they have to function in not only in good faith but in very good faith. But in the instant case, shareholders’ faith seemed to have been belied. It was a case where the respondents and the persons concerned in the management of the company appeared to be guilty of fraud, misfeasance and other misconduct towards the company. The business of the company appeared to have been carried on otherwise for a fraudulent and unlawful purpose. There was no force in the respondents’ plea that the condition precedent for exercising power under section 237(b) is that the business of the company should be in operation, i.e., the company should be carrying on operation and in case by any reason whatsoever has stopped carrying on the business, then no investigation can be carried out.

ruling.text:
The Court has also held that the absence of a statutory provision will not inhibit the Court (as the Court was exercising power under article 142 of the Constitution) while acting under the said article from making appro-priate orders for doing complete justice between the parties. The Court has also observed that in India, the Courts are not only Courts of law, but also the Courts of equity and thereafter passed an appropriate order attaching certain properties of the Directors of the company in the aforesaid case. 9. In our view, applying the aforesaid ratio and taking into consideration the fact that large number of investors are being defrauded by employing the corporate veil, which may amount to a criminal offence, and also the fact that under criminal law property acquired by fraud, cheating, etc., can be followed and seized from wherever it is lying, appropriate directions could be issued by this Court to search and seize such property. Further, under article 226, the Court can issue appropriate directions for not only enforcing any of the rights conferred by Part III but 'for any other purpose', and 'any other purpose' would include such public interest purpose.

10. In this view of the matter, we pass the following order:

