Notice to Central Government

25.9-10 The CLB 1 /Tribunal is required to give notice to the Central Government of every application made under section 397 or 398 to it, and is required to take into consideration any representation made to it by the Central Government before passing a final order (section 400).

Interim order [Section 403] 25.9-11 Pending the final order under section 397/398, on an application of any party to the proceeding, CLB/Trinunal is empowered to make any interim order in its discretion regulating the conduct of the company’s affairs upon such terms and conditions as appear to it just and equitable. [See, Polly Mathew v. Somatheeram Ayurvedic Beach Resort (P.) Ltd. (2005) 57 SCL 56 (CLB-Chennai) regarding questionable financial transactions in the conduct of company’s affairs and working and CLB’s powers to interfere under section 403]. In exercise of this power, CLB/Tribunal may also grant stay or ex parte stay - Rajeev Kwatra v. Sunil Khanna (2005) 60 SCL 184 (CLB-New Delhi).

CLB 1 /Tribunal’s Powers

25.9-12 Apart from the powers inhering in it and the language of the sections 397 and 398, the CLB 1/Tribunal has been conferred with the following powers specified in section 402/403/404/405/406/407/408/409 that may be exercised by its order :

u To pass interim orders on the application of any party for regulating the conduct of the company’s affairs;

u The regulation of the conduct of the company’s affairs in future;

u The purchase of the shares or interests of any members of the company by other members or by the company;

u In the case of purchase of its shares by the company, the consequent reduction of its share capital;

u The termination, setting aside or modification of any agreement however arrived at, between the company and the managing director, other director and the manager, upon such terms and conditions considered just and equitable in all the circumstances of the case, giving rise to no claim whatever against the company for damages or compensation for loss of office or otherwise, irrespective of such provision having been made in the agreement, and with the effect of debar against them for being reappointed by the company for five years, unless permitted by special leave of the Tribunal after notice to Central Government under section 400/407(1)(b)/(3) under section 407(1)(b).

u Identical powers as in the preceding matter in respect of any agreement with any other person only after due notice to, and modification only with the consent of, the party concerned;

u The setting aside of any transfer, delivery of goods, payment, execution or other act relating to property made or done by or against the company within three months before the date of the application under section 397/398, which would, if made or done by or against an individual, be deemed in his insolvency to be a fraudulent preference;

u Any other matter for which in the opinion of the Tribunal it is just and equitable that provision should be made;

u The making of any alteration in the memorandum or articles with the same efficacy of the company doing the same and to restrain the company from reversing the same without the Tribunal’s permission;

u Addition of the managing or any other director, manager or any other person as a respondent, for sufficient cause;

u Section 406 makes provision for the application of sections 539 to 544, as modified and specified in Schedule XI to the Act, in the proceedings under sections 397 and 398. The said sections deal with offences by officers of a company for falsification of books, frauds, failure to keep proper accounts, fraudulent conduct of business and the power of the CLB 1/Tribunal to assess damages against such delinquent officers including the directors of a body corporate and partners of a firm if those are involved in the offences committed as well as imposition of penalties. Rules 11(a), 18 and 19 of the Companies (Court) Rules apply for the procedure. In Colaba Land and Mills Co. v. Vasant Corporation (1963) 2 Comp. LJ 89 (Guj.), it was mentioned that separate proceedings under section 406/543 (in Sch. XI) would have to be initiated against the persons found guilty of misfeasance, breach of trust or fraud in the course of proceedings under section 397/398. A contrary view was held in Life Insurance Corporation v. Haridas Mundra (1966) 2 Comp. LJ 371 (DB) (All.) that the Court may grant relief under the said sections with no separate application needed. It does not appear that the matters of imposition of penalties and assessment of damages dealt by Sch. XI came up for consideration by the Courts while dealing with the applications under section 397/398 in many cases reported. It is therefore unclear about the efficacy of Sch. XI in connection with applications for the prevention of oppression and mismanagement.

