u (f ) direct any intermediary or any person associated with the securities market in any manner not to dispose of or alienate an asset forming part of any transaction which is under investigation;

u (g ) the measures mentioned at (d), (e) and (f ) may be applied by SEBI to any listed public company or the one intending to get its securities listed where SEBI has reasonable grounds to believe that the company in either case has been indulging in insider trading or fraudulent and unfair trade practices relating to securities market.

18.16-1e SEBI’s enquiry & directions - The important thing about these measures is that the opportunity of being heard, which it must be given to the alleged violators, either before or after such orders are issued by SEBI. If SEBI is satisfied after making or causing an enquiry that it is necessary, in the interest of investors or orderly development of securities market, or to prevent the affairs of any intermediary or other person associated with securities market being conducted in a manner detrimental to the interest of investors or securities market, or to secure the proper management of any such intermediary or person, it may issue directions to the intermediary or other person, and to any company in respect of matters specified in section 11A, as may be appropriate (section 11B).

18.16-1f Investigation by SEBI - If SEBI finds that reasonable ground exists to believe that the transactions in securities are being dealt with in a manner detrimental to the investors or the securities market, or any intermediary or other person associated with the securities market has violated any of the provisions of the SEBI Act/Rules/Regulations/directions issued by SEBI, it may order an Investigation of the affairs of the violator, to be carried out by the person, named in its order and to report on it. In the case of a company to be investigated in this behalf, it will be without prejudice to sections 235 to 241 of the Companies Act, and all officers and employees of the company or the intermediary or other person having the custody of all books, registers, other documents and record are duty bound to preserve and produce the same to the Investigator and answer his queries considered as relevant and necessary for purposes of the investigation by giving the requisite information or their explanations. If considered necessary, the Investigator may take and keep the books and papers in his custody for six months and return thereafter, subject to recall and grant of certified copies to the party if so required. The Investigator has been empowered to examine any person concerned with the affairs under investigation on oath the notes of which made by the Investigator have evidentiary value against the persons examined. For search and seizure of records suspected as being susceptible of being destroyed, mutilated, altered, falsified or secreted, and in the case of companies listed or to be listed where insider trading or market manipulation is indulged in, the Investigator has the power to do so, but before doing so he has to take out the order of the Judicial Magistrate of the first Class having jurisdiction (section 11C). SEBI has been vested with the power to order to cease and desist against the violation of the provisions of the Act done or caused by any person, and in the cases of companies listed or to be listed when SEBI has reasonable grounds to believe that insider trading or market manipulation is indulged in by such company, (section 11D).

Compulsory registration of intermediaries

18.16-2 Section 12 provides for the SEBI registration as compulsory for all intermediaries and prohibits them from carrying on any business otherwise than in accordance with the conditions of the certificate of registration issued by SEBI. Sections 30 and 29 empower SEBI to make and notify Regulations consistent with the SEBI Act, and for the Central Government to make and notify Rules. Application of other laws is not barred, section 32. [Regarding RTIs & STAs, see para 16.28]

SEBI General Fund

22.22 Investigation by SEBI

Investigating authority appointed by SEBI

22.22-1 To ascertain whether the provisions of SEBI Act and the Regulations are being complied with, or suo motu upon its own knowledge or information, or on receipt of complaints from investors/intermediaries/any person on any matter having a bearing on the allegations regarding substantial acquisition of shares and takeovers, SEBI may appoint one or more persons to investigate. Such an investigating authority appointed by SEBI may be any of its officers or any other person—not being a firm/body corporate/AOP—having experience with the problems relating to the security market. In such an eventuality, normally it is required that SEBI serves a ten-day notice on all or any of the acquirer, seller, target company or merchant banker, but where SEBI is satisfied that in the interest of investors no such notice should be given, it may by order in writing direct that such investigation be taken up without notice.

Production of books, securities accounts

22.22-2 The acquirer, seller, target company or the merchant banker whose affairs are being investigated into and every director, officer or employee connected with the party are duty bound, firstly, to produce such books, securities, accounts, records and other documents in their custody or control and submit written statements relating to their activities as required and in the time specified by the investigator; secondly, allow the investigator or his nominee’s personal inspection to the premises occupied and examination of any books, records, documents and computer data in their possession and provide copies of such as are considered in his opinion relevant for the investigation; thirdly, submit to examination in person and recording of their statements by the investigator; and fourthly, provide all assistance reasonably required by the investigator.

Interim report of investigator

22.22-3 If so required by SEBI, the Investigator shall submit interim reports or otherwise the final report on conclusion of the investigation, and SEBI in turn after consideration of the report(s) communicate its findings to the party or parties whose affairs have been investigated giving an opportunity of being heard.

Investigation by qualified auditor

22.22-4 Independent of this investigation process, SEBI may appoint a qualified auditor to investigate into the books of account or affairs of the party complained against and the auditor shall have the same powers as the investigator, and presumably same duty of submitting his report to SEBI.

SEBI taking measures deemed fit

22.22-5 In either case and in conclusion of this process, SEBI may call upon the party or parties to take such measures as deemed fit in the interest of the securities market and due compliance of the provisions of SEBI Act and the Regulations. Regulation 44 empowers SEBI, independent of its powers reserved under Chapter VIA and section 24 of SEBI Act to initiate civil and criminal actions respectively, and to issue directions as deemed fit in the interest of securities market/protection of interest of investors, and those directions may include:

u Directing to appoint a merchant banker for causing disinvestments of shares acquired in breach of Regulation 10, 11 or 12 either through public auction or market mechanism, in their entirety or in small lots or through offer of sale;

u Directing the transfer of any proceeds or securities to the investors’ protection Fund of a recognized stock exchange;

u Directing the target company or depository to cancel the shares where an acquisition of shares pursuant to an allotment was made in breach of Regulation 10,11 or 12;

u Directing the target company or the depository not to give effect to transfer or further freeze the transfer of any such shares and not to permit the acquirer or any nominee or any proxy of the acquirer to exercise any voting or other rights attached to such shares acquired in violation of Regulation 10, 11 or 12;

u Debarring any person concerned from accessing the capital market or dealing in securities for such period as may be determined by SEBI;

u Directing the person concerned to make public offer to the shareholders of the target company to acquire such number of shares at such offer price as determined by SEBI;

u Directing the disinvestments of such shares as are in excess of the percentage of the shareholding or voting rights specified for disclosure in Regulation 6, 7 or 8;

u Directing the person concerned not to dispose of assets of the target company contrary to the undertaking given in the letter of offer;

u Directing the person concerned, who has failed to make or delayed in making a public offer in terms of the Regulations, to pay the shareholders, whose shares have been accepted in the public announcement and offer after the delay, the consideration amount along with interest at the rate not less than the applicable rate of interest payable by banks on fixed deposits.

