Investigating authority appointed by SEBI

22.22-1 To ascertain whether the provisions of SEBI Act and the Regulations are being complied with, or suo motu upon its own knowledge or information, or on receipt of complaints from investors/intermediaries/any person on any matter having a bearing on the allegations regarding substantial acquisition of shares and takeovers, SEBI may appoint one or more persons to investigate. Such an investigating authority appointed by SEBI may be any of its officers or any other person—not being a firm/body corporate/AOP—having experience with the problems relating to the security market. In such an eventuality, normally it is required that SEBI serves a ten-day notice on all or any of the acquirer, seller, target company or merchant banker, but where SEBI is satisfied that in the interest of investors no such notice should be given, it may by order in writing direct that such investigation be taken up without notice.

Production of books, securities accounts

22.22-2 The acquirer, seller, target company or the merchant banker whose affairs are being investigated into and every director, officer or employee connected with the party are duty bound, firstly, to produce such books, securities, accounts, records and other documents in their custody or control and submit written statements relating to their activities as required and in the time specified by the investigator; secondly, allow the investigator or his nominee’s personal inspection to the premises occupied and examination of any books, records, documents and computer data in their possession and provide copies of such as are considered in his opinion relevant for the investigation; thirdly, submit to examination in person and recording of their statements by the investigator; and fourthly, provide all assistance reasonably required by the investigator.

Interim report of investigator

22.22-3 If so required by SEBI, the Investigator shall submit interim reports or otherwise the final report on conclusion of the investigation, and SEBI in turn after consideration of the report(s) communicate its findings to the party or parties whose affairs have been investigated giving an opportunity of being heard.

Investigation by qualified auditor

22.22-4 Independent of this investigation process, SEBI may appoint a qualified auditor to investigate into the books of account or affairs of the party complained against and the auditor shall have the same powers as the investigator, and presumably same duty of submitting his report to SEBI.

